1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,208
How much money does the merchant get today?

2
00:00:02,258 --> 00:00:07,608
In Lesson 1, ecom counted at the end of each day what the shop earned, and kept the bank's fee.

3
00:00:07,658 --> 00:00:12,504
This lesson builds that count, night by night, so every payout can be explained.

4
00:00:12,554 --> 00:00:18,480
At the end of the month, a merchant's accountant puts the bank's payouts next to the shop's own orders.

5
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:21,384
One week is off, and nobody can say why.

6
00:00:21,434 --> 00:00:23,640
Was last week's refund counted today?

7
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:26,136
Was a reversed payment counted as a sale?

8
00:00:26,186 --> 00:00:28,176
Did one night simply not run?

9
00:00:28,226 --> 00:00:31,536
A total that nobody can take apart cannot be trusted.

10
00:00:31,586 --> 00:00:33,504
Think of a shop at closing time.

11
00:00:33,554 --> 00:00:35,592
The owner counts the till for the day.

12
00:00:35,642 --> 00:00:37,920
Counting it again gives the same answer.

13
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:42,984
Two things make that count trustworthy: a clear day, and a count you can repeat.

14
00:00:43,034 --> 00:00:46,944
The bank does the same, every night, for each website.

15
00:00:46,994 --> 00:00:48,816
First, the clear day.

16
00:00:48,866 --> 00:00:53,016
A business day runs from midnight to midnight, in the bank's own time zone.

17
00:00:53,066 --> 00:00:59,784
It is the same day that decides, in Lesson 32, whether money goes back by reversal or by refund.

18
00:00:59,834 --> 00:01:05,424
So the reports and the refund rules never disagree about which day a payment belongs to.

19
00:01:05,474 --> 00:01:07,296
Then the count.

20
00:01:07,346 --> 00:01:11,640
Every night, the bank adds up each website's day in four numbers.

21
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:15,168
Paid is the payments made that day that were not reversed.

22
00:01:15,218 --> 00:01:19,920
Refunded is the refunds made that day, whenever the original payment was.

23
00:01:19,970 --> 00:01:23,640
The fee is the bank's share, from the fee rules of Lesson 22.

24
00:01:23,690 --> 00:01:30,216
And the payout is what the merchant receives: what was paid, less what was refunded, each after its fee.

25
00:01:30,266 --> 00:01:34,464
A day with many refunds and few sales gives a negative payout.

26
00:01:34,514 --> 00:01:36,336
That is not an error.

27
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:38,376
It is the honest answer for that day.

28
00:01:38,426 --> 00:01:40,248
Now the part you can repeat.

29
00:01:40,298 --> 00:01:42,480
Counting a day again is a rebuild.

30
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:46,104
A rebuild replaces the day's numbers; it never adds to them.

31
00:01:46,154 --> 00:01:51,384
So each night, the bank counts not only yesterday, but a few days before it again.

32
00:01:51,434 --> 00:01:55,104
If one night is missed, the next night simply catches up.

33
00:01:55,154 --> 00:01:57,264
Nothing is ever counted twice.

34
00:01:57,314 --> 00:02:05,112
And behind every total sits a list of every line: each payment, and each refund as a negative line.

35
00:02:05,162 --> 00:02:08,304
So any number can be explained, one line at a time.

36
00:02:08,354 --> 00:02:10,176
Let's watch the product.

37
00:02:10,226 --> 00:02:13,992
The merchant's admin opens the portal and chooses Reports.

38
00:02:14,042 --> 00:02:16,344
The page is called Daily reports.

39
00:02:16,394 --> 00:02:21,648
It explains that each night the bank adds up the day before for every website.

40
00:02:21,698 --> 00:02:24,984
There is one row for each website and business day.

41
00:02:25,034 --> 00:02:30,840
The columns read Business day, Paid, Bank fee, Refunded, and You receive.

42
00:02:30,890 --> 00:02:33,144
One day shows a payment and a refund.

43
00:02:33,194 --> 00:02:35,016
Paid shows the payment's amount.

44
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:36,888
Refunded shows the refund.

45
00:02:36,938 --> 00:02:39,480
You receive shows what is left after the fee.

46
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:44,832
On another day, refunds outweigh sales, and You receive shows a minus.

47
00:02:44,882 --> 00:02:47,184
The admin downloads that day's lines.

48
00:02:47,234 --> 00:02:49,344
The file opens in a spreadsheet.

49
00:02:49,394 --> 00:02:54,912
The payment is there, with its order, its website, its amount, its fee and its time.

50
00:02:54,962 --> 00:02:58,008
The refund is there too, as a negative line.

51
00:02:58,058 --> 00:03:02,184
The card column shows only the first six and the last four digits.

52
00:03:02,234 --> 00:03:05,040
Add up the lines, and you get the totals on the screen.

53
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:08,664
A shop with no payments yet sees an empty page instead.

54
00:03:08,714 --> 00:03:12,720
It says a report appears the morning after the first day with payments.

55
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:14,592
What does this protect?

56
00:03:14,642 --> 00:03:17,856
It protects the merchant from a payout they cannot check.

57
00:03:17,906 --> 00:03:23,136
It protects the bank's support team from arguing about a number nobody can take apart.

58
00:03:23,186 --> 00:03:30,288
And it protects both from the quietest mistake of all: a night that did not run, or a day counted twice.

59
00:03:30,338 --> 00:03:39,240
With a clear day, a rebuild that replaces, and every line behind every total, the accountant's question finally has an answer.

60
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:41,136
Back to the till at closing time.

61
00:03:41,186 --> 00:03:46,968
One clear day, counted the same way every time, and every coin accounted for.

62
00:03:47,018 --> 00:03:50,040
Next: when a buyer says, I never bought this.
